<article class="env-lemma" id="0122">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0122">Lemma <span data-tag="0122">12.19.2</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. The category of filtered objects $\text{Fil}(\mathcal{A})$ has the following properties: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
It is an additive category. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
It has a zero object. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
It has kernels and cokernels, images and coimages. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
In general it is not an abelian category. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It is clear that $\text{Fil}(\mathcal{A})$ is additive with direct sum given by $(A, F) \oplus (B, F) = (A \oplus B, F)$ where $F^ p(A \oplus B) = F^ pA \oplus F^ pB$. The kernel of a morphism $f : (A, F) \to (B, F)$ of filtered objects is the injection $\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(f) \subset A$ where $\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(f)$ is endowed with the induced filtration. The cokernel of a morphism $f : A \to B$ of filtered objects is the surjection $B \to \mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(f)$ where $\mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(f)$ is endowed with the quotient filtration. Since all kernels and cokernels exist, so do all coimages and images. See Example <a href="/tag/0108" data-tag="0108">12.3.13</a> for the last statement. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>